=== commit message head
KS-823: the refresh_token grant authenticates the client and honours a client binding on OAuth-minted tokens

The client-authentication block installed for the authorization_code grant sat
entirely inside that branch. The refresh branch read no client_id, resolved no
app and never reached verifyClientSecret -- so a CONFIDENTIAL app's refresh
token needed no credential, a DEACTIVATED app's refresh token kept working while
its codes stopped, and nothing checked that the party redeeming a refresh token
was the client it was issued to. A token-binding weakness: the branch still
required a VALID refresh token, so a caller with nothing got a 400; what the
defect bought was that a refresh token, once obtained, could be redeemed by any
party.

The fix, on the same terms as the code grant (RFC 6749 s6 with s2.3.1):
- the refresh branch resolves the client -- client_id from the body, or the
  userid half of an HTTP Basic header (readClientId) -- through the same definer
  lookup as the code grant, which pins is_active (one 401 invalid_client for
  unknown and deactivated); a confidential app must present its secret (Basic
  or body, the existing constant-time check); a public app presents client_id
  and no secret. The client is authenticated BEFORE the token is verified.
- OAuth-minted tokens carry the client they were issued to: jwt.ts gains
  OAuthMintOptions / OAuthTokenClaims (an intersection type -- JwtPayload in
  types/index.ts is untouched) and generateTokenPair / generateAccessToken /
  generateRefreshToken take an optional third argument that puts `client_id` on
  both halves. The code grant mints bound to the redeeming client; the refresh
  grant re-mints bound to the presenting client.
- the refresh grant refuses a token whose `client_id` claim does not name the
  presenting client, or carries none (400 invalid_grant). "None" is fail-closed
  by decision: a token minted before binding existed, or by the interactive
  login flow (which never passes through this grant), is not redeemable here.
  The cost is one re-authorization; the alternative is a token redeemable by
  anyone who holds it. The alternative (grandfather unbound tokens when the
  client authenticates) is named in the PR for a ruling, not taken.
- the SCOPE comment in the code-grant block (written by #825, amended by #831)
  said the refresh branch authenticated no client and recorded that as an open
  defect; rewritten to describe the branch as it is now, keeping the reason the
  sentence existed.

Tests (services/auth, vitest run, node v24.7.0, darwin):
- NEW ks823-refresh-grant-client-auth-and-binding.test.ts, 11 cells over a raw
  socket with the REAL jwt.ts (initJwtKeys awaited; tokens minted in-process by
  generateTokenPair and verified by the real verifyRefreshToken): no credential
  -> 401; wrong secret -> 401; deactivated app with its right secret -> 401;
  wrong client redeeming another's token -> 400 invalid_grant; unbound token by
  an authenticated client -> 400 invalid_grant; no client identification -> 400
  invalid_request; MINT -- the code grant's pair carries client_id on both
  halves and the refresh grant's re-issued pair keeps it. Controls: right
  secret in the body -> 200; right secret over Basic (client_id from the Basic
  userid) -> 200; a public app with its client_id and no secret -> 200; an
  invalid token by an authenticated client -> 400 invalid_grant (the existing
  invalid-token catch survives).
- Red-first at the parent commit (the unfixed product): Tests 7 failed | 4
  passed (11) -- every refusal cell answered 200 with a token, and the minted
  pair carried no client_id; the four controls green. Green 11/11 after.
- Whole auth suite 52 files / 702 tests (was 51 / 691 on the parent). tsc -p
  services/auth rc 0; the new test file type-checked separately rc 0.
- Tampers (unique anchors, restored by content, sha asserted): T1 client auth
  skipped on refresh -> 2 red (no credential; wrong secret); T2 the binding
  check removed -> 2 red (wrong client; unbound token); T3 the code grant mints
  without the client -> 1 red (MINT); T4 the null-app refusal removed -> 1 red
  (deactivated). 4/4 exact.
- ks790-token-pre-auth-user-lookup.test.ts (this lane's, stacked): its refresh
  cases now present the client credential and a bound stub token, as the grant
  requires; assertions unchanged.

Not covered: a live refresh grant on any environment; the four platform suites;
which deployed clients hold OAuth refresh tokens today (the re-authorization
cost of the fail-closed arm is unmeasured -- stated in the PR).

